<!-- ======================================================= EXECUTIVE SUMMARY -->
<h1 class="unnum" id="s-exec">Executive summary</h1>
<p>Two SOC problems on CSE-CIC-IDS2018 [1], on Wednesday brute force (FTP, SSH) and Thursday infiltration: which classifier to deploy (Task 1), and whether attacks can be found without labels (Task 2). All splits are time-ordered on a repaired clock.</p>
<div class="keybox">
  <ul>
    <li><b>Task 1 answer: Random Forest (RF) for attack families with labelled history.</b> This model raises <b>{{ n.t3_wed80_rf_fa }}</b> false alerts per day ({{ n.c_ci }} CI {{ n.t3_wed80_rf_fa_lo }}–{{ n.t3_wed80_rf_fa_hi }}) on a brute-force campaign it has seen, and that is acceptable because it costs about {{ n.w80_hours }} analyst-hours per day at {{ n.c_min }} min per alert ({{ n.w80_shift }} of one {{ n.c_shift }}-hour shift) for {{ n.w80_recall }} recall. On Thursday infiltration the same model raises {{ n.t3_thu70_rf_fa }} false alerts per day, which is not acceptable (Section 5).</li>
    <li><b>ML does not catch an unseen attack type; a rule does.</b> On {{ n.sp_wed70 }} the SSH attack was absent from training and RF recall was {{ n.t3_wed70_rf_recall }}, while the port 21/22 rule catches all of it (recall {{ n.t3_wed70_port_recall }}). The rule was written knowing the ports, so this favours it.</li>
    <li><b>Clustering separates brute force without labels.</b> DBSCAN reaches F1 {{ n.wed_db_f1_4 }} on Wednesday for {{ n.t2_wed_dbscan_labels }} analyst labels. On infiltration all three detectors miss {{ n.t7_missed_share }} of the attack flows and a one-line zero-payload rule matches their F1 ({{ n.thu_pay_fd_f1 }} against {{ n.thu_best_det_f1 }}). The only finds no rule reproduces are the GMM-only ({{ n.t7_onlygmm_n }}) and DBSCAN-only ({{ n.t7_onlydb_n }}) long, near-silent connections.</li>
    <li><b>Caveats.</b> The {{ n.t3_wed80_rf_fa }}/day rests on {{ n.t3_wed80_rf_fp }} false positives, and {{ n.sp_wed80 }} shares one SSH campaign between training and test (an optimistic bound). A random split inflates Thursday RF F1 by only {{ n.t4_thu_delta_rf }}; the unrepaired clock distorts far more.</li>
    <li><b>The ceiling.</b> {{ n.t7_allatk_dup }} of sampled infiltration flows are feature-identical to some benign flow, so flow-level detection of infiltration is capped.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<!-- ======================================================= 1 METHODOLOGY -->
<h1 id="s1">Methodology</h1>
<h2 id="s1-1">Data and cleaning</h2>
<p>Both days were cleaned in the same order: repeated header lines, rows dated {{ n.epoch }} and rows holding ±Infinity were removed, then constant columns were dropped (<a class="ref" href="#tab-1">Table 1</a>). NaNs occurred only in rows that also held Infinity ({{ n.p_wed_nanbefore }} and {{ n.p_thu_nanbefore }} rows). Duplicate flows were counted and kept: identical flows are legitimate repeated traffic. <i>Timestamp</i> and <i>Dst Port</i> were dropped as features (time reveals the attack window; ports {{ n.port_21 }} and {{ n.port_22 }} almost define the Wednesday labels); <i>Protocol</i> was kept. The label is binary. Thursday has no {{ n.epoch }} rows.</p>
<div class="tabwrap" id="tab-1">
  <p class="tcap">Data cleaning and class balance per day. Rows removed add up exactly to rows in file minus rows kept.</p>
  <table class="bk">
    <thead><tr><th class="l">Step</th><th>Wednesday 14-02-2018</th><th>Thursday 01-03-2018</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
    {%- for r in T1 %}
      <tr><td class="l">{{ c(r.label) }}</td><td>{{ c(r.wed) }}</td><td>{{ c(r.thu) }}</td></tr>
    {%- endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>

<h2 id="s1-2">Clock repair and the honest split</h2>
<p>The raw hour histogram holds traffic in hours {{ n.h_a1 }}–{{ n.h_a2 }} and {{ n.h_b1 }}–{{ n.h_b2 }} and none in hours {{ n.h_g1 }}–{{ n.h_g2 }}, which fits a {{ n.clk_shift }}-hour clock written without AM/PM (the afternoon is stored as {{ n.h_a1 }}–{{ n.h_a2 }}). SSH brute force appears at raw {{ n.clk_wed_ssh_rf }}–{{ n.clk_wed_ssh_rl }} and becomes {{ n.clk_wed_ssh_ff }}–{{ n.clk_wed_ssh_fl }} after adding {{ n.clk_shift }} hours. The repair moved {{ n.p_wed_moved }} Wednesday and {{ n.p_thu_moved }} Thursday flows and leaves one daytime window per day ({{ n.p_wed_span }} h and {{ n.p_thu_span }} h). The evidence is data-internal only: the UNB schedule page could not be fetched, so the published schedule is not verified, and file order is not chronological ({{ n.p_wed_back }} backward steps on Wednesday).</p>
<p>The first {{ n.frac_wed70 }} of flows in time train and the last {{ n.frac_30 }}% test (<a class="ref" href="#fig-1">Figure 1</a>, <a class="ref" href="#tab-2">Table 2</a>). {{ n.sp_wed70 }} tests SSH, never seen in training, so it measures unseen-attack behaviour, not leakage. We added {{ n.sp_wed80 }} after seeing that result, to test a known attack arriving later; its training and test windows share one SSH campaign, so it is an optimistic bound. {{ n.sp_thu70 }} cuts the second infiltration wave. <a class="ref" href="#tab-4">Table 4</a> shows why the split matters.</p>
<figure id="fig-1">
  <img src="{{ FIG }}/fig1_timeline.png" alt="Flows per five minutes by label after the clock fix">
  <figcaption><b>The repaired clock gives each attack a compact window, and the cuts fall inside them.</b> Flows per {{ n.c_block }} min by label (log scale) after the clock fix, with the {{ n.frac_wed70 }} cut ({{ n.sp_wed70 }}, {{ n.t1_wed70_cut }}), the {{ n.frac_wed80 }} cut ({{ n.sp_wed80 }}, {{ n.t1_wed80_cut }}) and the {{ n.frac_thu70 }} cut ({{ n.sp_thu70 }}, {{ n.t1_thu70_cut }}). Top: Wednesday, where FTP trains and SSH tests. Bottom: Thursday, two infiltration waves.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div class="tabwrap" id="tab-2">
  <p class="tcap">Time-ordered splits. Validation window = last {{ n.c_valfrac }} of the training rows, used for tuning only.</p>
  <table class="bk">
    <thead><tr><th class="l">Split</th><th>Cut</th><th class="l">Training attacks</th><th class="l">Test attacks</th><th>Test window (h)</th><th class="l">Validation-window attacks</th></tr></thead>
