<h2 id="s1-3">Task 1 configuration and evaluation</h2>
<p><b>Models and preprocessing.</b> LogReg and Random Forest (RF; min_samples_leaf {{ n.cfg_rf_leaf }}) with balanced class weights, and an MLP with early stopping [2]; seed {{ n.c_seed }}. Final settings on {{ n.sp_wed70 }} / {{ n.sp_wed80 }} / {{ n.sp_thu70 }}: LogReg C = {{ n.t1_wed70_ch_lr }} / {{ n.t1_wed80_ch_lr }} / {{ n.t1_thu70_ch_lr }}; RF {{ n.t1_wed70_ch_rft }} / {{ n.t1_wed80_ch_rft }} / {{ n.t1_thu70_ch_rft }} trees; MLP layers {{ n.t1_wed70_ch_mlp }} / {{ n.t1_wed80_ch_mlp }} / {{ n.t1_thu70_ch_mlp }}. A signed log (no fitted parameters) is followed by a StandardScaler fitted on training rows only; threshold {{ n.c_thr }}.</p>
<p><b>Tuning protocol.</b> The last {{ n.c_valfrac }} of the training rows form a time-ordered validation window. Grids: C {{ n.g_lr0 }}/{{ n.g_lr1 }}/{{ n.g_lr2 }}; RF trees {{ n.g_rft0 }}/{{ n.g_rft1 }}/{{ n.g_rft2 }} × leaf {{ n.g_rfl0 }}/{{ n.g_rfl1 }}/{{ n.g_rfl2 }}; MLP {{ n.g_mlp_a }}, {{ n.g_mlp_b }}-{{ n.g_mlp_b2 }}, {{ n.g_mlp_c }}-{{ n.g_mlp_c2 }}. A split is tuned only if its validation window has at least {{ n.c_minatk }} attacks <i>and</i> they are of a type in the fit part. {{ n.sp_wed70 }} was tuned (validation holds {{ n.t1_wed70_valatk }} FTP flows; F1 varies by at most {{ n.t1_wed70_sens_max }}) and {{ n.sp_thu70 }} was tuned (RF spread {{ n.t1_thu70_sens_rf }}; all families at most {{ n.t1_thu70_sens_max }}). {{ n.sp_wed80 }} was not: its {{ n.t1_wed80_valatk }} validation SSH flows were never seen by the fit part ({{ n.t1_wed80_fitatk }} FTP flows), every setting scores validation F1 of at most {{ n.t1_wed80_sens_top }}, so defaults were kept.</p>
<p><b>Metrics.</b> Attack-class precision, recall, F1 and AP. F1 carries a {{ n.c_ci }} block-bootstrap interval ({{ n.c_block }}-minute blocks, {{ n.c_boot }} resamples) [8]; false-positive counts carry an exact Poisson {{ n.c_ci }} interval [10]; model pairs use McNemar's test [4] (exact if b + c &lt; {{ n.c_mcn }}). <b>False alerts per day (Task 1)</b> = benign flows flagged ÷ test-window hours × {{ n.c_day }}.</p>

<h2 id="s1-4">Task 2 configuration and evaluation</h2>
<p><b>Setup.</b> Labels were set aside first. A systematic sample of {{ n.t2_n }} flows per day (every {{ n.t2_wed_step }}th Wednesday, {{ n.t2_thu_step }}th Thursday flow) was signed-log transformed, scaled and reduced to {{ n.t2_npca }} PCA components. <b>K-Means</b> [9]: k {{ n.g2_k_lo }}–{{ n.g2_k_hi }} × {{ n.g2_km_seeds }} seeds, silhouette [6] on {{ n.t2_sil }} rows. <b>GMM</b>: {{ n.g2_gmm_lo }}–{{ n.g2_gmm_thu_hi }} components ({{ n.g2_gmm_wed_hi }} on Wednesday), full or diagonal covariance, BIC [7]. <b>DBSCAN</b> [5]: {{ n.g2_eps_n }} eps values from the k-distance curve × min_samples {{ n.g2_ms0 }}, {{ n.g2_ms1 }}, {{ n.g2_ms2 }}, {{ n.g2_ms3 }}, silhouette over settings with at most {{ n.g2_noise_cap }} noise; noise is triaged as its own cluster. A simulated analyst labels each cluster by the majority of {{ n.t2_triage }} random flows; labels spent are the cost. Extra sweeps vary PCA dimensions, sample size, triage size and analyst error. Results are seed means (K-Means {{ n.t2_km_seeds }}, GMM and DBSCAN {{ n.t2_gmm_seeds }} seeds). <b>Baselines:</b> the port {{ n.port_21 }}/{{ n.port_22 }} rule alerts on destination port {{ n.port_21 }} or {{ n.port_22 }}; the zero-payload rule alerts when forward and backward payload bytes are both zero and duration is below {{ n.pl_dur }} µs.</p>
<p><b>False alerts per day (Task 2) use the measured span, not {{ n.t2_cap }} h.</b> The reported value is sample false-positive rate × the day's benign flows × 24 ÷ {{ n.t2_cap }}; we rescale it as <i>reported × {{ n.t2_cap }} ÷ measured span</i>. The flows span {{ n.t2_wed_span }} h (Wednesday) and {{ n.t2_thu_span }} h (Thursday), so both tasks use the traffic rate in the data. Task 1 and Task 2 false-alert numbers have different denominators and are never compared in one row.</p>

<h2 id="s1-5">Reproducibility</h2>
<p>One command (<i>run_all.sh</i>) rebuilds results, figures and this PDF; <i>scripts/check_report_numbers.py</i> checks every printed number against <i>results/report_registry.json</i>. Repository: <a href="https://github.com/MZU-GH/SEC-635-Lab-1">github.com/MZU-GH/SEC-635-Lab-1</a>.</p>

<!-- ======================================================= 2 RESULTS -->
<h1 id="s2">Results and visualizations</h1>
<figure id="fig-2">
  <img class="full" src="{{ FIG }}/fig2_confusion.png" alt="Confusion matrices">
  <figcaption><b>RF is near-perfect on a seen campaign ({{ n.t3_wed80_rf_fp }} false positives, {{ n.t3_wed80_rf_fn }} misses on {{ n.sp_wed80 }}) and unusable on Thursday.</b> Confusion matrices on the time-ordered test windows (rows: true label; cell shading is log-scaled).</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>On {{ n.sp_wed70 }} every model catches the same {{ n.t3_wed70_rf_tp }} of {{ n.t1_wed70_testatk }} SSH flows. On {{ n.sp_thu70 }} RF flags {{ n.thu_benign_flagged }} of benign flows ({{ n.t3_thu70_rf_fp }}) and misses {{ n.thu_miss }} of attacks ({{ n.t3_thu70_rf_fn }}); the MLP trades recall ({{ n.t3_thu70_mlp_recall }}) for precision.</p>
<p>On {{ n.sp_wed70 }} LogReg scores SSH below {{ n.c_thr }}, yet a threshold set for {{ n.br_budget_hi }} false alerts per day would reach {{ n.br_wed70_lr_10000 }} recall. On {{ n.sp_thu70 }} all curves approach the chance level of {{ n.t1_thu70_chance }} (AP {{ n.t3_thu70_lr_ap }}–{{ n.t3_thu70_rf_ap }}). On Wednesday the detectors recover both attack groups; on Thursday most infiltration flows (<a class="ref" href="#fig-4">Figure 4</a>, pink) sit on benign clusters, and a 2-D projection of {{ n.t2_npca }} components cannot show overlap, so <a class="ref" href="#tab-7">Table 7</a> measures it in full feature space.</p>
<figure id="fig-3">
  <img src="{{ FIG }}/fig3_pr.png" alt="Precision-recall curves">
  <figcaption><b>LogReg ranks unseen SSH well (AP {{ n.t3_wed70_lr_ap }}) yet its {{ n.c_thr }} threshold misses it: thresholds matter.</b> Attack-class precision–recall curves; chance level (attack share) dotted.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="fig-4">
  <img src="{{ FIG }}/fig4_clusters.png" alt="Clusters on the first two principal components">
  <figcaption><b>Wednesday attacks form compact groups detectors isolate; Thursday attacks lie inside the benign cloud.</b> {{ n.t2_plot }} of the {{ n.t2_n }} sampled flows per day (random subsample) on PC1/PC2; left: true labels; others: detector result.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="fig-5">
  <img src="{{ FIG }}/fig5_sweeps.png" alt="Parameter sweeps for every Task 2 algorithm">
  <figcaption><b>Wednesday F1 sits on a wide plateau, but Thursday F1 stays low except at extreme settings, and the label-free choice (star) is rarely the best value.</b> Attack F1 after triage per parameter (top Wednesday, bottom Thursday); K-Means band: min–max over {{ n.g2_km_seeds }} seeds; DBSCAN: one line per min_samples.</figcaption>
</figure>

<p>K-Means F1 flattens after the chosen k. The Wednesday GMM plateaus early, yet its BIC kept improving to the grid edge ({{ n.t2_wed_gmm_k }} components): an edge, not an optimum. DBSCAN drops at large eps on both days, where clusters merge, and on Thursday no setting exceeds F1 {{ n.t2_thu_db_fmax }}.</p>

<!-- ======================================================= 3 METRIC COMPARISONS -->
<h1 id="s3">Metric comparisons</h1>
<h2 id="s3-1">Task 1: supervised classifiers</h2>
<div class="tabwrap" id="tab-3">
  <p class="tcap">Task 1, attack class, time-ordered test windows. FP = benign flows flagged. False alerts/day = FP ÷ test hours × {{ n.c_day }}, with exact Poisson {{ n.c_ci }} CI. Bold: best F1 and fewest false alerts per split. Rules have no score, so no AP.</p>
  <table class="bk">
    <thead><tr><th class="l">Split / model</th><th>Precision</th><th>Recall</th><th>F1</th><th>F1 {{ n.c_ci }} CI</th><th>AP</th><th>FP</th><th>False alerts/day [{{ n.c_ci }} CI]</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
    {%- for g in T3 %}
      <tr class="grp"><td colspan="9">{{ g.name }}</td></tr>
      {%- for r in g.rows %}
      <tr><td class="l ind{% if r.rule %} rule-row{% endif %}">{{ r.model }}</td><td>{{ c(r.prec) }}</td><td>{{ c(r.rec) }}</td><td{% if r.f1_bold %} class="b"{% endif %}>{{ c(r.f1) }}</td><td>{{ c(r.ci) }}</td><td>{{ c(r.ap) }}</td><td>{{ c(r.fp) }}</td><td{% if r.fa_bold %} class="b"{% endif %}>{{ c(r.fa) }}</td></tr>
      {%- endfor %}
    {%- endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>
<p><b>Wednesday.</b> On {{ n.sp_wed70 }} the models are equally blind (recall {{ n.t3_wed70_rf_recall }}) while the port rule scores F1 {{ n.t3_wed70_port_f1 }}. On {{ n.sp_wed80 }} RF and MLP are indistinguishable (F1 {{ n.t3_wed80_rf_f1 }}, {{ n.t3_wed80_mlp_f1 }}), the port rule matches them on detection (F1 {{ n.t3_wed80_port_f1 }}), and RF's measured advantage is false alerts: {{ n.t3_wed80_rf_fa }} per day against {{ n.t3_wed80_port_fa }}, with non-overlapping intervals. <b>Thursday.</b> No model works: RF F1 {{ n.t3_thu70_rf_f1 }}, LogReg {{ n.t3_thu70_lr_f1 }}, MLP {{ n.t3_thu70_mlp_f1 }}, at {{ n.t3_thu70_mlp_fa }}–{{ n.t3_thu70_rf_fa }} false alerts per day; the port rule catches {{ n.thu_port_rec_t1 }} of infiltration and the zero-payload rule (F1 {{ n.t3_thu70_pay_f1 }}) is no better than the best classifier.</p>

<div class="tabwrap" id="tab-4">
  <p class="tcap">Does the split matter? F1 under a random split, the honest time split and (Thursday) the unrepaired clock, run for RF only. Thursday time-split cells carry the {{ n.c_ci }} block-bootstrap CI.</p>
  <table class="bk">
    <thead><tr><th class="l">Evaluation</th><th>LogReg</th><th>RF</th><th>MLP</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
    {%- for r in T4 %}
      <tr><td class="l">{{ c(r.label) }}</td>{% for cell in r.cells %}<td>{{ c(cell) }}</td>{% endfor %}</tr>
    {%- endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>
<p><b>The split matters little on Wednesday and moderately on Thursday; the clock matters most.</b> On Wednesday random-split inflation is negligible (F1 differs by at most {{ n.t4_wed_delta_lr }}) because brute force is trivially separable. On Thursday all three models score higher on a random split (LogReg +{{ n.t4_thu_delta_lr }}, RF +{{ n.t4_thu_delta_rf }}, MLP +{{ n.t4_thu_delta_mlp }}), yet each random value lies inside its time-split CI, so the direction is consistent but the size is uncertain. The unrepaired clock is the largest distortion: Thursday RF scores {{ n.t4_thu_unrep_rf }}, outside the time-split CI [{{ n.t4_thu_ci_rf_lo }}, {{ n.t4_thu_ci_rf_hi }}]. The earlier comparison against {{ n.sp_wed70 }} mixed leakage with an unseen attack type (RF F1 {{ n.t4_wed70_rf }}) and is withdrawn.</p>
<p><b>Significance</b> (McNemar; b = flows only the first model gets right, c = only the second). {{ n.sp_wed80 }}: RF vs MLP is a tie (b = {{ n.mc_wed80_rf_mlp_b }}, c = {{ n.mc_wed80_rf_mlp_c }}, {{ n.mc_wed80_rf_mlp_p.p }}); both beat LogReg ({{ n.mc_wed80_lr_rf_p.p }}); RF beats the port rule ({{ n.mc_wed80_port_rf_p.p }}), with {{ n.mc_wed80_port_rf_b }} errors against {{ n.mc_wed80_port_rf_c }}. On {{ n.sp_wed70 }} pairs differ only through a few false positives ({{ n.mc_wed70_lr_rf_p.p }}). {{ n.sp_thu70 }}: every pair differs ({{ n.mc_thu70_lr_rf_p.p }}, {{ n.mc_thu70_rf_mlp_p.p }}, {{ n.mc_thu70_port_rf_p.p }} for RF vs the port rule), but McNemar treats flows as independent and the block-bootstrap CIs overlap widely (RF [{{ n.t4_thu_ci_rf_lo }}, {{ n.t4_thu_ci_rf_hi }}], MLP [{{ n.t4_thu_ci_mlp_lo }}, {{ n.t4_thu_ci_mlp_hi }}]), so the ranking is weak evidence.</p>

