<h2 id="s4-3">Limits</h2>
<p>The analyst is assumed error-free in the headline results (error of {{ n.sw_analyst_error_0 }}–{{ n.sw_analyst_error_2 }} moves F1 by at most {{ n.sw_err_max }}). Daily counts assume a constant rate over {{ n.t2_wed_span }} h and {{ n.t2_thu_span }} h spans. The port rule was written with hindsight. The GMM grid uses a single seed; each sample is {{ n.t2_n }} flows. {{ n.sp_wed80 }} is an optimistic bound. The attack schedule was not verified externally.</p>

<!-- ======================================================= 5 PRODUCTION -->
<h1 id="s5">Production recommendation</h1>
<p><b>Random Forest on Wednesday-like brute force: this model raises {{ n.t3_wed80_rf_fa }} false alerts per day ({{ n.c_ci }} CI {{ n.t3_wed80_rf_fa_lo }}–{{ n.t3_wed80_rf_fa_hi }}), and here is why that is acceptable:</b> at {{ n.c_min }} minute per alert it costs {{ n.w80_hours }} analyst-hours per day ({{ n.w80_shift }} of one {{ n.c_shift }}-hour shift; {{ n.w80_hours_hi }} hours at the upper CI bound) while it catches {{ n.w80_recall }} of a campaign it has seen.</p>
<p><b>Random Forest on Thursday infiltration: this model raises {{ n.t3_thu70_rf_fa }} false alerts per day, and here is why that is not acceptable:</b> that is {{ n.thu_hours }} analyst-hours per day, or {{ n.thu_analysts }} analysts on {{ n.c_shift }}-hour shifts, and it still catches only {{ n.thu_recall }} of infiltration flows. A validation-chosen threshold still gives {{ n.thu_op_fa }} per day at {{ n.thu_op_recall }} recall.</p>
